faq:
  - q: "Should we put a chatbot on the site?"
    a: "Only if it's genuinely good and there's an obvious route to a human. A bot that answers simple questions well and hands over quickly is fine. A bot that loops customers through irrelevant answers costs more goodwill than it saves in wages — and customers remember it."
  - q: "Will customers know a reply was AI-drafted?"
    a: "Not if an agent has reviewed and adjusted it, because at that point it's the agent's reply. What people detect is generic, over-formal writing that ignores what they asked — which is a review problem, not an AI one."
  - q: "What's the biggest risk?"
    a: "Confidently stating a policy that isn't yours. AI will invent a plausible refund window if it doesn't know the real one. Ground replies in your actual documented policies and make agents check anything committing you to something."
  - q: "Does it work for phone support?"
    a: "Indirectly and well — summarising the account and recent history before the call, and drafting the follow-up after. Live call handling is a different, much harder proposition."
  - q: "How do we keep replies sounding like us?"
    a: "A system prompt with your tone rules and three real examples of good replies does most of it. Vague instructions like 'be friendly' produce the generic register everyone recognises."
